// CONFORMANCE-CASE
// spec: 21-standard-library-semantics
// facility: rule.stdlib.x509 [Verify-Chain] (D-0160); conf.x509-chain-too-long (D-0211, CHG-0249)
// kind: positive
// expect: ok
// slow: interpreted big-number arithmetic: two chains of ten P-256 signatures take minutes
// expect-stdout-hex: 696e7465726d656469617465733a2031300a6e696e6520646565703a206f6b0a74656e20646565703a20636572746966696361746520636861696e20746f6f206c6f6e670a
//
// The chain-length limit: verify_chain follows at most ten certificates
// from the leaf. A PKI made with openssl (stress/hostile_oct11/chain/): a
// root, ten intermediates each signed by the one before, and two leaves
// for deep.example, one under the ninth intermediate (nine intermediates
// between it and the root: verifies) and one under the tenth (ten:
// `ChainTooLong`). The limit bounds the work a hostile chain can demand.

